<article class="env-corollary" id="6FA7">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/6FA7">Corollary <span data-tag="6FA7">4.2.4.7</span>.</a> Let $\calI $ be a fibrant simplicial category, $S$ a simplicial set, and $p: \sNerve (\calI ) \rightarrow S$ a map. Then it is possible to find the following: </p>
  <p><ul>
  <li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(1)$"><p>
A fibrant simplicial category $\calC $. 
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(2)$"><p>
A simplicial functor $P: \calI \rightarrow \calC $. 
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(3)$"><p>
A categorical equivalence of simplicial sets $j: S \rightarrow \sNerve (\calC )$. 
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(4)$"><p>
An equivalence between $j \circ p$ and $\sNerve (P)$, as objects of the $\infty $-category $\Fun ( \Nerve (\calI ), \sNerve (\calC ))$. 
</p>
</li>
</ul></p>
</article>
<article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Choose an equivalence $i: \sCoNerve [S] \rightarrow \calC _0$, where $\calC _0$ is fibrant; let ${\mathbf A}$ denote the model category of simplicial presheaves on $\calC _0$ (endowed with the <em>injective</em> model structure). Composing $i$ with the Yoneda embedding of $\calC _0$, we obtain a fully faithful simplicial functor $\sCoNerve [S] \rightarrow {\mathbf A}^{\circ }$, which we may alternatively view as a morphism $j_0: S \rightarrow \sNerve ({\mathbf A}^{\circ })$. 
    <p>
      We now apply Proposition <a href="/tag/A3AB" data-tag="A3AB">4.2.4.4</a> to the case where $u$ is the counit map $\sCoNerve [\sNerve (\calI )] \rightarrow \calI $. We deduce that the natural map 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  \sNerve ({\mathbf A}^{\calI })^{\circ } \rightarrow \Fun ( \Nerve (\calI ), \sNerve ({\mathbf A}^{\circ } ) ) \]
</div>
    <p>
       is an equivalence. From the essential surjectivity, we deduce that $j_0 \circ p$ is equivalent to $\sNerve (P_0)$, where $P_0: \calI \rightarrow {\mathbf A}^{\circ }$ is a simplicial functor. 
    <p>
      We now take $\calC $ to be the essential image of $\sCoNerve [S]$ in ${\mathbf A}^{\circ }$, and note that $j_0$ and $P_0$ factor uniquely through maps $j: S \rightarrow \sNerve (\calC )$, $P: \calI \rightarrow \calC $ which possess the desired properties. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>